@*
    Whether the galaxy may dress a fallen empire in this design.

    Not "play as a fallen empire", which no design can ask for. The game builds a fallen empire from
    scratch in common/fallen_empires/00_fallen_empire.txt - its country type, its imperial authority,
    origin_fallen_empire and civic_empire_in_decline are each gated playable = { always = no } or
    country_type = { value = fallen_empire } - and a design marked yes and then played is an ordinary
    empire.

    What it marks is eligibility to be the *appearance* of an AI one: species, portrait, name, flag,
    ship prefix and room are taken, and the design's own ethics, civics and origin are thrown away.
    It fires on a 5% roll (FALLEN_CUSTOM_EMPIRE_SPAWN_CHANCE = 50 in 00_defines.txt) and it overrides
    spawn_enabled, which is why the Blorg Commonality carries spawn_enabled = no beside it.

    The game writes the field into every design it saves, always no, and offers no control for it -
    there is no localisation key and no .gui element anywhere. So this is the only place it can be
    set, and the wording has to carry all of the above, because "spawn as fallen" reads as something
    far larger than it is.
*@

<button type="button" class="sem-toggle" aria-pressed="@(Fallen ? "true" : "false")"
        title="A fallen empire the galaxy generates may wear this design's species, portrait, name and flag. It does not change the empire you play."
        @onclick="() => Session.Edit(d => d.SpawnAsFallen = d.SpawnAsFallen != true)">
    @(Fallen ? "Allowed" : "Disallowed")
</button>

@code {
    [CascadingParameter]
    public required DesignSession Session { get; set; }

    private bool Fallen => Session.Current!.SpawnAsFallen == true;
}
